# Multiplicity at most one for each signed cuspidal eigenpacket

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.signed_packet_multiplicity_one`
(`54e086dd-490e-4713-b7a9-af8adc243460`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `b27f7470-d6da-4827-8325-0b6ce7b2da42`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Any two compactly supported cohomology classes with the same sign, nebentype and full prime
eigenpacket of the given cuspidal eigenform are linearly dependent. This is a separate
Eichler–Shimura/multiplicity-one obligation: it is not inferred from injectivity alone. It includes
arbitrary eigenforms at the stated level, not only newforms.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Signed multiplicity one from the unsigned span

We reduce the statement

$$
\phi,\psi\in H_c(N,k-2;\mathbf C)\ \text{with the same sign, nebentypus and full prime eigenpacket
of }f\ \Longrightarrow\ \phi,\psi\ \text{are linearly dependent}
$$

to two lemmas about the reflection $\mathcal R$ induced by $\operatorname{diag}(-1,1)$ and to the
existence of the integration map. The children are assumptions of this submission, not results of
it.

## The children

1. `MTT.Cohomology.integration_map` (existing milestone): there is an injective, Hecke-equivariant
   linear map $I:S_k(\Gamma_1(N))\to H_c$ whose classes satisfy the coefficient-evaluation formula.
   Only the equivariance and evaluation properties are used here.

2. `MTT.Cohomology.packet_span` (new): if $\phi\in H_c$ satisfies $T_\ell\phi=a_\ell\phi$ for every
   prime $\ell$ (with $U_\ell$ at primes dividing $N$) and the nebentype law for $\varepsilon$, then
$$\phi=c\,x+d\,\mathcal R x\qquad\text{for some }c,d\in\mathbf C,\quad x:=I(f).$$
This is the Eichler–Shimura decomposition of $H_c$ into holomorphic classes, antiholomorphic classes
and boundary symbols, combined with multiplicity one for the full packet in $S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$ and
the exclusion of the cuspidal packet from the boundary. It carries no sign condition.

3. `MTT.Cohomology.reflection_involutive` (new): $\mathcal R(\mathcal R\phi)=\phi$ for every cochain
   $\phi$.

## The reduction

Write $x=I(f)$ and $y=\mathcal R x$, so that $\mathcal R y=x$ by the involution lemma, and let
$\sigma\in\{\pm1\}$ be the common sign, so that $\mathcal R\phi=\sigma\phi$ and $\mathcal
R\psi=\sigma\psi$. By `packet_span`, applied to the first two conjuncts of the packet condition,
$$\phi=c\,x+d\,y,\qquad \psi=c'\,x+d'\,y .$$
The reflection is additive and homogeneous, so applying it to $\phi$ and using the eigen-relation
gives
$$c\,y+d\,x=\sigma c\,x+\sigma d\,y,\qquad\text{i.e.}\qquad (d-\sigma c)\,x+(c-\sigma d)\,y=0,$$
and likewise for $\psi$.

There are three cases. If $x=0$, then $\phi=\psi=0$ and the pair $(a,b)=(1,0)$ works. If $y=\lambda
x$ for some $\lambda$, then $\phi=(c+d\lambda)x$ and $\psi=(c'+d'\lambda)x$ are multiples of one
vector, and $(a,b)=(c'+d'\lambda,\,-(c+d\lambda))$ works, with the fallback $(1,0)$ when both
coefficients vanish. Otherwise $x$ and $y$ are linearly independent, and the displayed relation
forces $d=\sigma c$ and $d'=\sigma c'$, so
$$\phi=c\,(x+\sigma y),\qquad \psi=c'\,(x+\sigma y),$$
and $(a,b)=(c',-c)$ works, again with the fallback $(1,0)$ when $c=0$.

In every case the pair is nontrivial and $a\phi+b\psi=0$, which is the required linear dependence.
No non-vanishing of $L$-values is needed for this direction: independence of $x$ and $\mathcal R x$
is exactly what would make both signed eigenspaces nonzero, and the argument above does not require
it.

